# Paper and Documentation Standards

## Mathematical Notation

Use consistent LaTeX notation throughout all documentation:

| Concept                  | Notation                                      |
|--------------------------|-----------------------------------------------|
| Mean direction           | $\boldsymbol{\mu}$                            |
| Concentration            | $\kappa$                                      |
| Temperature              | $\tau$                                        |
| CLIP embedding           | $\mathbf{z}$                                  |
| fMRI input               | $\mathbf{x}$                                  |
| Loss function            | $\mathcal{L}$                                 |
| Real space               | $\mathbb{R}^d$                                |
| Unit hypersphere         | $\mathcal{S}^{d-1}$                           |
| vMF density              | $f(\mathbf{x}; \boldsymbol{\mu}, \kappa)$     |
| Bessel function          | $I_v(\kappa)$                                 |
| Normalizing constant     | $C_d(\kappa)$                                 |
| Guidance scale           | $s$                                           |
| Number of subjects       | $N_{\text{subj}}$                             |

## Citation Format

- In-text: Author et al., Year (e.g., "Allen et al., 2022")
- Multiple: (Allen et al., 2022; Scotti et al., 2024)
- Never use bare numbers like "[1]" in Markdown docs — always author+year
- Maintain a central reference list in the paper outline

## Reporting Experimental Results

### Mandatory Elements
- **Sample size**: Always state N (number of test samples, subjects, etc.)
- **Statistical tests**: Report test statistic, p-value, and effect size (Cohen's d)
- **Confidence intervals**: When possible, report 95% CI
- **Source traceability**: Every number must link to a specific file in `outputs/` or `RaportPaper3/`

### Table Format
```markdown
| Method       | Cosine↑ | R@1↑  | R@5↑  | p-value | Cohen's d |
|-------------|---------|-------|-------|---------|-----------|
| Baseline     | 0.XXX   | X.XX% | X.XX% | —       | —         |
| Ours (Full)  | 0.XXX   | X.XX% | X.XX% | < 0.001 | X.XX      |
```

- Always indicate metric direction (↑ = higher is better, ↓ = lower is better)
- Report to consistent decimal precision (3 decimals for cosine, 2 for percentages)
- Bold the best result in each column

### Figures
- Reference format: "Figure X: [descriptive caption]"
- Every figure must have a caption that is understandable without reading the text
- Use vector formats (SVG/PDF) for plots, not rasterized images
- Consistent color scheme across all figures

## Never Fabricate Results

This is the most important rule. If an experiment hasn't been run:
- Write "**Pending experiment**" explicitly
- Do not estimate, extrapolate, or guess what the numbers might be
- Reference the config file that would run the experiment: "See `configs/experiments/exp7_vmf_nce.yaml`"

## Document Structure

### Paper Outline (`docs/paper/outline.md`)
- Follow standard ML paper structure: Abstract → Introduction → Related Work → Method → Experiments → Discussion → Conclusion
- Each section has bullet points for key arguments
- Mark sections as [DRAFT], [COMPLETE], or [NEEDS DATA]

### Ablation Plan (`docs/paper/ablation_plan.md`)
- Each experiment has: ID, hypothesis, config file, metrics to report, status (done/pending)
- Results filled in only when actual numbers exist

## Writing Style

- Active voice preferred: "We propose" not "It is proposed"
- Present tense for established facts: "CLIP embeddings lie on the unit hypersphere"
- Past tense for experiments: "We trained the model for 100 epochs"
- Precise language: "improves" requires statistical evidence; use "aims to improve" for untested claims
- Define all acronyms on first use: "Natural Scenes Dataset (NSD)"
